---
/*
 * /learn/what-is-business-process-automation — Glossary playbook for the
 * broader "automation" query cluster.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";

const title = "What Is Business Process Automation? A Plain Guide | Arkansas Automated";
const description =
  "Business process automation means software does the repeatable handoffs: capturing leads, chasing quotes, scheduling, invoicing. What it is, what to automate first, and what to leave alone.";

const site = "https://arkansasautomated.com";
const pageUrl = `${site}/learn/what-is-business-process-automation`;
const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: "What Is Business Process Automation?",
      description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Andre Brassfield", jobTitle: "Founder, Arkansas Automated" },
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Arkansas Automated", url: site },
      datePublished: "2026-07-29",
      dateModified: "2026-07-29",
      mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${site}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Learn", item: `${site}/learn` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "What is business process automation?", item: pageUrl },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/learn/what-is-business-process-automation" schema={schema} ogType="article">
  <SiteNav />
  <main>
    <article class="hero">
      <div class="container">
        <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/learn">Learn</a><span>/</span>What is business process automation?
        </nav>
        <h1 class="heading-xl measure">What is business process automation?</h1>
        <p class="body-lg measure" style="margin-top:1.15rem">
          Business process automation means software handles the repeatable handoffs in your
          operation: capturing the lead, chasing the quote, booking the job, sending the
          invoice, and asking for the review. The steps happen the same way every time, on
          time, without anybody remembering to do them. It is not about replacing people. It
          is about stopping the leaks between them.
        </p>
        <p class="fine-print" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Last updated July 2026. Written by Andre Brassfield, founder of Arkansas Automated.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <section class="section section-tint">
      <div class="container article-body">
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">The handoffs where small businesses leak</h2>
        <p>
          Almost every leak in a small service business sits between two people or two systems.
          The call reaches voicemail between the customer and the office. The quote waits
          overnight between the office and the estimator. The job details get typed three times
          between the field, the CRM, and accounting. The follow-up runs on memory between the
          estimate and the second touch. None of these are people problems. They are handoff
          problems, and handoffs are what automation is for.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What to automate first</h2>
        <p>
          In the order that pays back fastest for most Arkansas businesses: answering every
          call, because a missed call is a customer dialing the next name on the list. Quote
          and estimate follow-up, because the second and third touch close a third of open
          quotes and belong to nobody today. Scheduling, because phone tag costs an hour a job.
          Invoice chasing, because late invoices are a cash-flow problem wearing a politeness
          costume. Then review requests, because your reputation should compound while you work.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What to leave alone</h2>
        <p>
          Judgment, negotiation, pricing exceptions, and the conversations that decide whether
          somebody trusts your company stay with your people. Good automation makes that line
          obvious instead of blurring it. If a system cannot state plainly where it stops and
          a human starts, it is not finished. Our own rule, printed on our homepage and applied
          to every build: we will tell you what not to automate before the invoice, not after.
        </p>

        <div class="next-step">
          <p>
            Want your own leaks mapped with dollar figures attached? The
            {" "}<a class="text-link" href="/services/operations-audit">$999 operations audit</a>
            {" "}does exactly that, and the fee credits toward any build within 60 days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="link-chips" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
          <a class="chip" href="/learn/what-is-an-ai-receptionist">What is an AI receptionist?</a>
          <a class="chip" href="/for">Automation by industry</a>
          <a class="chip chip-strong" href="/learn">All guides</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
